The import didn't fail? It imported world.js which imports three.module.js, which requires no DOM at module level. Good.

Now player.js, audio.js, ui.js(? maybe main), main.js, index.html, styles.css.

Player:

```js
import * as THREE from '../vendor/three.module.js';

export class Player {
  constructor(world) {...}
  setPosition(x,y,z)
  update(dt, input, camera)
}
```
Maybe keep camera in main and player provides position/rotation. I'll implement controls in main to simplify.

Player physics class:
```js
export class Player {
  constructor(world) {
    this.pos = new THREE.Vector3();
    this.vel = new THREE.Vector3();
    this.yaw = 0; this.pitch = 0;
    this.onGround = false; this.inWater = false; this.flying = false;
    this.width = 0.62; this.height = 1.8; this.eye = 1.62;
    this.walkTime = 0;
  }
  spawn(x,z) { const y = world.surfaceY(x,z) + 1.1; this.pos.set(x+0.5, y, z+0.5); }
  aabbCollides(x,y,z) {...}
  update(dt, input, world) {
     // input: {forward, strafe, jump, sprint, up, down}
  }
}
```
Physics details:
- Determine inWater at feet or mid.
- Flying: vertical from jump/up; no gravity; speed 10, sprint 18.
- Walking: acceleration: desired velocity components from yaw. Use exponential smoothing:
```
const accel = onGround ? 14 : 4;
vel.x += (desiredX - vel.x) * min(1, accel*dt);
```
- gravity 30; jump 8.4. In water: gravity 8, upward when jump 4, drag on vel; max fall 3.
- Step collision with substeps: compute movement; break into steps of max 0.25 along each axis; for each axis apply then resolve.
- Auto-jump? No.

Collision function:
```js
collidesAt(x, y, z) {
  const half = this.width/2;
  const minX = Math.floor(x-half), maxX = Math.floor(x+half);
  const minY = Math.floor(y+0.001), maxY = Math.floor(y+this.height-0.001);
  const minZ = ...
  for y.. for x.. for z.. if (world.isSolidAt) return true;
  return false;
}
```
Feet at y. If y is 24.999 due to floating and floor is block at 24, floor(24.999)=24 → solid → collides → can't stand. Need epsilon: treat y+0.001. Typically pos.y equals top of block exactly (25.0), floor(25.001)=25 → air. Good. When falling and y = 24.9 → floor(24.9)=24 → collides → push up. Resolution axis-by-axis:

```
moveAxis(delta, axis) {
  if (delta===0) return;
  this.pos[axis] += delta;
  if (this.collides()) {
     // step back
     this.pos[axis] -= delta;
     if (axis==='y') {
       if (delta < 0) { // landed: snap to top of block
          // find nearest integer above
          this.pos.y = Math.ceil(this.pos.y); // approximate
          this.onGround = true;
       }
       this.vel.y = 0;
     } else this.vel[axis] = 0;
  }
}
```
Snapping via Math.ceil works when falling small distances; for high speeds use substeps. With substeps of 0.2, ceil is safe. For jumping into ceiling, snap down: Math.floor(pos.y + height) - height.

Better implement substeps:
```
const steps = Math.ceil(Math.max(|dx|,|dy|,|dz|)/0.2);
for i<steps: move with delta/steps each axis
```
On y collision down: set pos.y = Math.floor(pos.y + 1e-4)? Hmm if pos.y = 24.97 after collision step, the block top is 25. Set pos.y = Math.ceil(pos.y - 1e-6)? pos=24.97 → ceil = 25. Good. But if colliding at 24.0 exactly (block at 23?) Actually floor(24.0001)=24; the blocking block occupies y=24..25? Wait if feet at 24.0, the block at y=24 is solid → collides; the top of the blocker is 25. Hmm confusing. Let's say ground block is at y=24 (its top surface y=25). Player standing has feet at 25.0. If falling, feet go to 24.95 → block y=24 (floor 24.95 = 24) is solid → collision; restore to 25.0 via ceil(24.95)=25. Correct.

If player jumps and hits ceiling: feet y=27.5, head 29.3; block at y=29 solid → collide; snap pos.y = floor(29.3) - 1.8 = 29 - 1.8 = 27.2? The block bottom is 29, so head should be at 29 → feet 27.2. Use Math.floor(pos.y + height) - height - 1e-6. Fine.

Horizontal: stepping up? no.

Also plant blocks are not solid; glass solid yes; water non-solid.

Spawn: surfaceY + 1.05 maybe aligns feet above ground. surfaceY returns top solid block y; feet should be at y+1. Use +1.02.

Camera: pos + eye height + bob.

Held item bob/swing in main.

Input abstraction:
```
const keys = new Set()
keydown: keys.add(e.code)
```
Touch: joystick vector; separate.

Mouse look sensitivity 0.0022 rad/px. pitch clamp ±89°.

DDA raycast:
```js
export function raycastVoxel(world, origin, dir, maxDist=7, hitWater=false) {
  let x=floor(ox), y=..., z=...
  const stepX = sign(dir.x)...
  tMaxX = ...; tDeltaX = abs(1/dir.x)
  nx=ny=nz=0
  for (i=0;i<200;i++){
    const id = world.getBlock(x,y,z);
    if (id && id !== B.WATER) return {x,y,z,nx,ny,nz,id};
    // step
  }
  return null
}
```
Standard Amanatides. Careful with dir components 0 → Infinity.

Include plants as hittable. Water pass-through.

Break/place in main.

Particles: implement in main with a pool:
```js
class Particles {
  constructor(scene) { group; list=[]; max 60; }
  spawn(x,y,z, blockId, count=12)
  update(dt)
}
```
Each particle: Mesh(blockGeometry(id), mat) with scale 0.12-0.2, velocity, life 0.7, rotation speed. Materials: create per-material basic/lambert? Use Lambert to be lit; atlas map + vertexColors? blockGeometry has no color attribute, Lambert without vertexColors works. Use one material per block? A single MeshLambertMaterial with map atlas works for all (UVs differ). Set material.vertexColors false. Particles scale and rotate; geometry cached. Dispose? shared cache, don't dispose.

Pooling meshes per particle creation okay for short bursts.

Held item: Mesh(blockGeometry(id), heldMaterial with map, side? use MeshLambertMaterial but to avoid fog use MeshBasicMaterial with vertexColors? blockGeometry no colors. Use MeshBasicMaterial map, transparent? no. Add to camera; depthTest false; renderOrder 100. Set scale 0.34. position (0.4,-0.32,-0.62) rotation (0, -0.6, 0.15). On block change, swap geometry. Animate bob: base pos + sin(walk)*0.02, swing rotation on use.

To keep the held item lit consistently use MeshBasicMaterial with color slightly gray. Add a simple "hand" maybe not.

Held item from camera: camera added to scene, mesh child; since camera is in scene it's fine. Depth test false ensures always visible; but geometry might be clipped by near plane at 0.1 and distance 0.62 fine. FOV 75.

Underwater overlay: #tint div with background rgba(20,70,160,.35), pointer-events none, opacity transition.

Damage/health? Not needed.

Falling out of world: if y < -10 respawn.

Save: localStorage.

UI module maybe all in main to reduce files. main.js will be long (~800 lines). Split: ui.js with HUD/inventory/menu wiring and main.js with game. But UI requires game callbacks. I'll write main.js with UI setup (DOM exists in HTML).

